4. Replace throw; with throw e;
- throw; rethrows the same exception object, preserving its original type.
- throw e; throws a copy of e. This may cause object slicing if the exception was derived from another exception class.
Therefore, throw; is preferred when rethrowing an exception.
5. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
Instead of throwing exceptions, invalid claims could be rejected using std::optional<double>. A valid claim returns a value, while an invalid claim returns std::nullopt.
Invalid Claims	Exceptions	std::optional
0%	Very low overhead	Very low overhead
1%	Slight overhead for exceptions	Efficient
50%	High overhead due to frequent exceptions	More efficient

Time Complexity: Both approaches are O(n) for processing n claims in the normal case. However, exceptions are more expensive when frequently thrown.

Space Complexity: Both require O(1) extra space per result. std::optional stores the value and a validity indicator, while exceptions use exception-handling storage when thrown.

Conclusion: Exceptions are suitable for rare and unexpected errors, while std::optional is more efficient when invalid claims are expected frequently.
